REFERENCE IMAGE INPUT

- Attach the user-provided Loadout screenshot as the primary composition and
  material reference. Match its 16:9 landscape composition: a full-bleed scenic
  background, compact top header, three side-by-side dark iron panels, carved
  wood/metal accents, gold selected borders, persistent bottom selection
  summary, and a large START MATCH action.
- Use existing Urkaaaz biome backgrounds and unit/ammunition images as
  secondary content references when available. Do not redraw those assets.
- The screenshot is a style reference only. Its invented game content,
  selected state, and displayed quantities are not Urkaaaz data.

HARD REQUIREMENTS

- Generate one static Android landscape-screen mockup, 1920 x 1080 px, aspect
  ratio 16:9.
- Show screen content only: no phone frame, no hand, no presentation board,
  no alternate variants, no captions outside the UI, and no watermark.
- This is a layout/design reference. Do not treat the output as a production
  bitmap or flatten controls and text into a runtime background.
- Preserve the screenshot's visual direction: painterly campaign landscape,
  layered dark iron/wood panels, restrained metal rivets, warm gold highlights,
  readable pale text, and strong separation between the interface and scene.
- Keep text, card art, status badges, panel surfaces, and background visually
  separable so developers can rebuild the screen with native Android views and
  individual resources.
- Use only Urkaaaz content listed below. Do not invent units, ammunition,
  spells, prices, unlocks, or game rules.

SCREEN CONTENT

Header:
- Back action on the left and title "LOADOUT".
- Subtitle "CAMPAIGN · LEVEL 06", matching the attached reference.
- A compact "GOLD" balance readout on the right with no plus button or purchase
  action. Show the balance as a dynamic UI value, not generated artwork; do not
  invent a balance amount.
- Keep the title centered in the upper scenic header, with the back action and
  balance aligned to the far left and right as in the reference.

MAIN CONTENT:
- Use three equal-width panels across the screen, ordered UNITS, AMMUNITION,
  SPELLS from left to right.
- Keep each panel's title and selection count visible above its content.
- Each panel's item cards form a horizontal carousel: show three cards at once
  and allow left/right swipes to browse the rest of that category. Do not
  shrink every catalog item into one static row or move the catalogs to
  vertical lists.
- In all three carousels, show selected and available options first, followed
  by unavailable, locked, and out-of-stock options. Preserve catalog order
  within each group and refresh the order when selection or progression status
  changes.
- Use the same card proportions, illustration area, title/status treatment,
  selected border, and separate "i" affordance in all three carousels.
- Show selection with a bright, glowing gold card border and a dark-centered
  gold check badge with a matching glow in the upper-right corner instead of
  the visible word "SELECTED". Keep the separate "i" control in the upper-left
  so the controls never overlap. Avoid a flat, non-glowing yellow outline.
- Every card must use the same fixed width and height across all categories;
  longer labels must truncate or wrap within the card rather than resize it.
- Keep any category-specific controls compact and outside the item cards.
- Keep the footer summary and START MATCH action visible at the bottom without
  scrolling.

UNITS:
- Use only DEFENDER, SAPPER, DEMOLISHER, RAGING BOAR, and SLINGMASTER.
- Do not show a unit purchase cost, supply-cost badge, resource icon, or
  currency amount on a unit card. Unit selection has no Loadout purchase cost.
- Unit names are UI text, not part of generated artwork.
- Show three portrait cards side by side in the horizontal carousel, with selected,
  unselected, and optionally disabled states. Selection is an illustrative
  design state only. Use a sample count of "2 / 3 SELECTED" to match the
  reference; real counts come from the current draft.
- The visible cards are a viewport into the actual five-unit catalog; do not
  imply that only three unit types exist.
- Give each card a small, visually separate circular "i" information button.
  Tapping the card body selects it; tapping "i" opens its item-details screen.
  Do not make the information button look like a selection checkmark.

AMMUNITION:
- Use one horizontal carousel, styled exactly like the UNITS and SPELLS
  carousels; do not add a second, lower carousel or a separate slot picker.
- Show ROCK first as a selected, fixed card representing slot 1. It cannot be
  deselected or replaced.
- Show the other ammunition types as ordinary selectable cards. Tapping one
  toggles it into or out of the next available optional slot; do not show a
  separate active-slot prompt or require a slot-selection step.
- The loadout still has exactly three slots total, including fixed ROCK.
- The other valid types are SIEGE BOMB, POWDER BARREL, FIRE RAIN, and
  PLAGUE CAULDRON. Tapping an already-selected type removes it from its slot.
- ROCK and SIEGE BOMB are unlimited. Do not print fabricated quantities.
  Finite stock values are supplied dynamically by the app and must not be
  invented in this mockup.
- Do not use the fictional FIRE ROCK or SPIKE BALL from the reference.
- Give every ammunition card a small, separate "i" information button that
  opens the shared ammunition-details screen without changing selection.

SPELLS:
- Use only FORTRESS SHIELD, ICE TRAP, RUNIC BASTION, HASTE, HEAL,
  CATAPULT LOCK, SLOWING FIELD, VAMPIRIC BANNER, STONE SKIN, SIEGE FRENZY,
  and BERSERKER.
- Spell names, gold costs, lock badges, and selection counts are native UI
  overlays. Do not bake them into the artwork.
- Use a sample count of "2 / 3 SELECTED" to match the reference; real counts
  and lock states come from the current draft and progression.
- The visible cards are a viewport into the actual spell catalog; do not imply
  that only three spell types exist.
- Place all spell choices in the horizontal carousel and allow left/right
  swipes to reach cards beyond the first three.
- Selected/locked states in the mockup demonstrate visual states only. Do not
  claim that a spell is unlocked for a specific level or profile.
- Do not use the fictional FIRE STRIKE, WIND BLAST, or EARTH SHIELD.
- Give each spell card a small, separate "i" information button that opens the
  shared spell-details screen without changing selection.

Footer:
- A persistent summary with live-style counters for UNITS, AMMO, and SPELLS.
- A prominent gold START MATCH button.
- Do not add a shop button, gold-purchase plus button, tutorial, or result UI.
- Do not show invented stock figures such as "8 / 10" or "3 / 10".

ASSET REUSE AND SEPARATION

- Use the supplied background reference only for atmosphere. In the real app,
  the background will be selected from the existing level-biome backgrounds.
- Use the five full-card unit illustrations generated with
  `loadout-unit-card-art.agp` when supplied. Their character identities must
  match the five existing Urkaaaz unit icons; do not change battlefield
  sprites or animation art.
- Match existing Urkaaaz art for the five ammunition types; do not generate
  replacement projectile art.
- Keep panels, selected borders, lock/stock markers, labels, and counters as
  separate native UI layers. Existing Android assets include the dashboard
  header panel, portrait frame, back button, and HUD panel.
- Render each card's "i" affordance as a separate native control. It must be
  distinct from selection and route to the shared full-screen detail page.
- Spell illustrations will be generated separately under the spell-card-art
  AGP.

NEGATIVE REQUIREMENTS

- No fictional content from the reference screenshot.
- No made-up currency, prices, inventory counts, unlock rules, or card names.
- No spell gameplay animation or battlefield effects presented as implemented.
- No text, labels, logo, watermark, signature, or interface baked into card art.
- No giant flattened background containing the UI.
- No extra buttons, tutorials, store links, AI controls, or campaign-progress
  indicators.
- The per-card "i" detail affordances are the only additional controls beyond
  the described Loadout layout.

OUTPUT

Return exactly one 1920 x 1080 landscape design mockup. Use it only to guide
native screen composition; do not import it as a single Android drawable.
